{
 "id": "L7",
 "n": 7,
 "title": "Making plans",
 "goal": "Set a date and time, invite and decline politely, and keep in touch by phone and message.",
 "notes": [
  {
   "h": "Months and dates",
   "p": "Months are a Sino-Korean number plus {{월}}: {{일월}}, {{이월}}, {{삼월}} and so on. Two months are irregular: June is {{유월}} (not six + {{월}}) and October is {{시월}} (not ten + {{월}}). Days of the month are a Sino-Korean number plus {{일}}: {{십오 일}} is the 15th. To ask the date, say {{몇 월 며칠이에요?}} The word for \"which day\" is always spelled {{며칠}}. Sound point: {{몇 월}} is said {{며둴}}, because the final consonant of {{몇}} carries over as a d sound.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "오늘 몇 월 며칠이에요?",
     "What's the date today?"
    ],
    [
     "유월 십오 일이에요.",
     "It's June 15th."
    ],
    [
     "제 생일은 시월 이십 일이에요.",
     "My birthday is October 20th."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Can and can't",
   "p": "Add {{-ㄹ 수 있어요}} to a verb stem ending in a vowel ({{가다}} becomes {{갈 수 있어요}}) and {{-을 수 있어요}} to a stem ending in a consonant ({{먹다}} becomes {{먹을 수 있어요}}). Swap {{있어요}} for {{없어요}} to say you can't. Never put {{못}} or {{안}} in front of {{있어요}} here. Sound point: the {{수}} is said with a tense s, {{쑤}}: {{갈 수 있어요}} sounds like {{갈 쑤 이써요}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "내일 갈 수 있어요.",
     "I can go tomorrow."
    ],
    [
     "오늘은 갈 수 없어요.",
     "I can't go today."
    ],
    [
     "몇 시에 올 수 있어요?",
     "What time can you come?"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "I'll do it: promises and offers",
   "p": "Use {{-ㄹ게요}} (after a vowel) or {{-을게요}} (after a consonant) when you promise or offer to do something for the other person: \"I'll text you\", \"I'll wait\". It is only for things you yourself will do. Compare {{-ㄹ까요?}}, which asks \"shall we / shall I?\", and {{-세요}}, which asks the other person to do it. Sound point: the {{게}} is said tense, {{께}}: {{보낼게요}} sounds like {{보낼께요}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "제가 문자 보낼게요.",
     "I'll text you."
    ],
    [
     "곧 갈게요.",
     "I'll be there soon."
    ],
    [
     "저는 이거 먹을게요.",
     "I'll have this one."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Inviting, saying no, and decided plans",
   "p": "Invite with {{같이}} (together) and {{-ㄹ래요?}} or {{-ㄹ까요?}}: {{같이 영화 볼래요?}} To say no kindly, apologize, give a reason with {{약속이 있어요}} (I have plans), and offer another time: {{다음에 같이 가요}}. When a plan is settled, use {{-기로 했어요}} \"(we) decided / agreed to\": {{만나기로 했어요}}. Sound point: {{같이}} is said {{가치}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "같이 영화 볼래요?",
     "Want to see a movie together?"
    ],
    [
     "미안해요, 그날은 약속이 있어요.",
     "Sorry, I have plans that day."
    ],
    [
     "토요일에 만나기로 했어요.",
     "We decided to meet on Saturday."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Phone and messages",
   "p": "{{전화}} is a phone call, {{문자}} a text message, {{카톡}} a KakaoTalk message (the app almost every Korean uses), and {{답장}} a reply. You {{보내다}} (send) a text and {{주다}} (give) someone a call: {{전화 주세요}}. Phone numbers are read digit by digit with Sino-Korean numbers, and zero is {{공}}. Sound points: {{문자}} is said {{문짜}}, {{약속}} is said {{약쏙}} and {{답장}} is said {{답짱}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "전화번호가 뭐예요?",
     "What's your phone number?"
    ],
    [
     "카톡 주세요.",
     "Message me on KakaoTalk."
    ],
    [
     "아직 답장이 없어요.",
     "There's no reply yet."
    ]
   ]
  }
 ],
